{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The reorder animation that dropped frames with ten items</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      You've got a sortable list, and when items reorder you want them to slide smoothly into their new positions instead of jumping instantly. The obvious approach: animate the <code>top</code> (or <code>left</code>) CSS property from the old position to the new one. It looks fine with three items. With thirty, it visibly stutters.
    </p>
    <p>
      The stutter isn't a bug in your animation code, it's that animating <code>top</code> forces the browser to recalculate the layout of everything around it, every single frame, for every item moving.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Any animated property that affects layout (<code>top</code>, <code>left</code>, <code>width</code>, <code>margin</code>) triggers a full layout recalculation on every animation frame. <code>transform</code> and <code>opacity</code> don't, the browser can animate them on the compositor thread without touching layout at all.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The fix isn't "animate faster," it's animating a fundamentally cheaper property while producing the exact same visual result. That's what the FLIP technique does: it lets the layout jump instantly to the new state, then fakes the animation entirely with a transform.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>First, Last, Invert, Play</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      FLIP stands for the four steps: measure the element's position <strong>F</strong>irst, let the DOM update to its <strong>L</strong>ast position instantly (no animation yet), calculate the difference and apply a transform to <strong>I</strong>nvert it back so it visually looks like it never moved, then remove that transform and let it transition, so it <strong>P</strong>lays from the old-looking position to the real new one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Concretely: read <code>getBoundingClientRect()</code> before the reorder (First), let the reorder happen in the DOM instantly (Last), compute <code>dx</code>/<code>dy</code> between First and Last and apply <code>transform: translate(dx, dy)</code> with no transition (Invert, makes it visually appear unmoved), then in the next frame remove the transform with a transition applied (Play). The browser only ever animates <code>transform</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This works because <code>transform</code> changes are handled entirely by the compositor when the element is already promoted to its own layer, no main-thread layout or paint work is needed per frame, regardless of how many other elements exist on the page. The technique doesn't reduce the amount of visual movement, it changes which part of the rendering pipeline has to do the work.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The kanban board that only stuttered in production</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team's drag-to-reorder kanban board animated card position by transitioning <code>top</code>. It was smooth in local testing with a handful of demo cards, then a real customer board with 80 cards visibly janked every time a card moved, since every other card in the column had to be re-measured on every frame of the animation.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Switching to FLIP, animating a <code>transform</code> computed from before/after <code>getBoundingClientRect()</code> snapshots, fixed the stutter without changing the visual result at all, the cards still slide into place, just via a cheap property instead of an expensive one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      They also found the fix mattered more on lower-end devices than their own dev machines suggested, main-thread layout work scales with both element count and device CPU speed, so the janky version passed unnoticed in local testing and only showed up clearly once real, varied hardware hit it in production.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

